Let me think carefully about the design content to make it concrete and internally consistent.

**Rationale section.** What is the question: does incorporating a chemical feedback mechanism (p-logP, QED, binding affinity injected during generation) (a) improve property scores, (b) reduce molecular hallucinations, (c) better align with real-world distributions preserving structural diversity (avoiding over-representation of ultra-long carbon chains)? The design must isolate the mechanism of feedback: in-generation learned feedback vs post-hoc selection by the same signal vs no signal.

**Controls section.** Numbered controls:
- Control 1 (Scoring-function identity): Arms B, C, D use identical frozen p-logP, QED, and binding-affinity oracle; same inputs (canonical SMILES/rdkit mol), same scaling (z-score or min-max computed once on the training set). The only manipulated difference is whether and when the signal enters generation. Arm A also evaluates with same functions but only for measurement (not used during generation).
- Control 2 (Backbone identity/match): one SMILES transformer, same architecture, tokenizer, init seed, epochs, batch order; only difference is generation policy.
- Control 3 (Selection vs feedback): Arm B is the "same-signal, different timing" control — post-hoc oracle-guided best-of-N selection from the Arm-A generator. This isolates whether in-generation feedback (C) adds anything beyond selection by the same signal.
- Control 4 (Matched sampling budget/seeds): all arms sample same # of molecules per seed at same temperature, same RNG seeds; matched training budget (same optimizer steps/epochs).
- Control 5 (Reward-term ablation, Arm D): baseline + feedback with reward = combined minus one property (or baseline + combined reward without hallucination guard). Actually Arm D in my plan: "baseline + oracle reward but WITHOUT the combined p-logP/QED/affinity reward terms". Let me reconcile: Arm D could be: same feedback mechanism as C but with reward weights on the three properties removed/zeroed to verify each component contributes. Or the plan said Arm D = baseline + oracle reward without combined reward terms. Let me define cleanly:

Arm definitions:
- Arm A: Baseline SMILES transformer, no property signal at generation.
- Arm B: Arm A generator + post-hoc best-of-K oracle selection (same frozen oracle). No in-generation feedback.
- Arm C: Arm A generator + in-generation learned feedback (policy-gradient RL, REINVENT-style reward = property-reward terms + KL-baseline-divergence penalty). Treatment.
- Arm D: Arm A generator + same feedback mechanism but reward excludes the three property terms (reward = KL term only / or each property term tested). Purpose: verify each property contributes and that feedback isn't just a stabilization artifact. Could be interpreted as "combined reward minus one property" — but simpler: Arm D uses the feedback mechanism with only the hallucination/diversity guard (KL to prior) and property reward set to zero; serves as a mechanism control that the observed C-vs-B differences are due to the property signal, not the reward-learning procedure itself.

Actually the researcher's requirement 3 mentions: "If your budget forces dropping one arm or sensitivity run, say in the document that it is dropped and why." So I can make a decision: given CPU budget, I might demote Arm D to a partial/optional arm or drop one sensitivity. Let me think about feasibility.

